    <%# == The slowest tests of the REPOSITORY, across this branch's window
        The wall-clock question at the grain the page has never had one. "Slowest tests" above ranks
        ONE run and its own comment says why it stays there: `example_id` is positional and not
        stable across refactors, so a ranking spanning runs on that key would pair rows not known to
        be the same test. That is a statement about the KEY. `spec_identity_id` is a different key —
        semantic, resolved out of band by `Ingest::IdentityResolver`, and stable across a move, a
        reorder and a reword alike — so the boundary that panel respects is not this one's, and
        `SlowestTests` is where the crossing is argued.

        The two panels are DELIBERATELY BOTH HERE and answer different questions. "Was that one bad
        run, or is this test chronically slow" cannot be read off a single run at any cap, and
        "which test cost the most in the run I just pushed" cannot be read off a window that sums
        thirty of them. They are separately identifiable on purpose: different id, different title,
        different file, and a first sentence in each that names its own grain.

        Drawn on the SAME window as "Suite growth", "Areas that grew or shrank over the window" and
        "Tests whose outcome changed" — same `?branch=` anchor, same `Repository::TRAJECTORY_LIMIT`,
        and literally the same loaded runs (see `RepositoriesController#show`). Branch-anchored
        because runtimes compared across branches are runtimes of different code; sharing the window
        because every panel that fetched "the last thirty runs on this branch" for itself would be
        its own window, with no structural reason to keep agreeing.

        ⭐ FOUR STATES AND NEVER ONE BLANK PANEL. `SlowestTests#state` decides which, and the
        wording lives here beside its siblings' so the panels cannot drift into describing the same
        window differently. The one that must not be got wrong is `:unresolved`: identity resolution
        runs out of band, so a run that landed seconds ago has rows nothing has matched yet, and an
        empty list over it reads as "nothing in this suite is slow" when what happened is "nobody
        has told us which tests these are". That is Vacuous Green verbatim, and it is the ordinary
        state for the moments after every ingest rather than a rare one.

        Outside the `manage_keys` gate, like every panel around it — read-only suite telemetry,
        nothing credential and nothing actionable. %>
    <% if @slowest_tests %>
      <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Slowest tests across the window",
                                        id: "slowest-tests-window", class: "lg:col-span-3") do %>
        <% case @slowest_tests.state %>
        <% when :ranked %>
          <%# What was ranked, over what window, by what rule, out of what population, and what the
              ranking could not reach — all before the list, because every one of them is a
              precondition for reading it rather than a note about it.

              Every figure comes off `@slowest_tests`, which holds the list and these counts
              together, so this paragraph cannot end up describing a different population from the
              rows below it. Nothing here is recomputed from the rows. %>
          <p class="text-xs text-app-muted mb-3" id="slowest-tests-window-basis">
            <%= slowest_tests_window_sentence(@slowest_tests) %>
            <%# ⭐ The partition: which tests are here at all was decided by ONE run, and a test the
                newest run did not report is absent however slow it was. Not recoverable from any
                row, so it is stated. %>
            <%= slowest_tests_anchor_sentence(@slowest_tests) %>
            <%# The matching rule — the one DECISION on this panel rather than a measurement, and
                the OPPOSITE of the rule the outcome panel states for itself. %>
            <%= slowest_tests_matching_sentence %>
            <%= slowest_tests_coverage_sentence(@slowest_tests) %>
            <%# The two silences: rows the anchor wrote that are not yet matched to a test, and
                candidates the cap never examined. Rendered only where there are any — a clause
                reading "0 rows carried no durable identity" is a sentence about arithmetic rather
                than about this window. %>
            <%= slowest_tests_exclusion_sentence(@slowest_tests) %>
          </p>
          <%# No `#any?` guard, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. `:ranked` is reached
              only where the anchor has resolved rows, which is exactly the condition under which
              `SpecObservation.slowest_identity_candidates_in` returns candidates and the
              composition over them returns groups — the model destructures `candidates.first`
              unconditionally on the same guarantee. An `else` here would be a branch no state
              reaches, and this page's empty states are load-bearing enough that a decorative one
              would be the worse mistake. %>
          <%= render UI::TableComponent.new(columns: ["Test", "Total across window",
                                                      "Slowest single run", "Runs seen", "Timed"],
                                            describedby: "slowest-tests-window-basis") do %>
            <% @slowest_tests.rows.each do |test| %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content">
                  <%# The descriptions this test was RECORDED under across the window, not a name
                      looked up on `spec_identities` — the composition deliberately joins nothing,
                      because what a reader recognises is the text their own runs reported.

                      `#descriptions` is a SET, sorted so two rows carrying the same one read the
                      same way, and there is therefore no "current" description at this grain: the
                      aggregate has no ordering to give one. So where a test wore more than one, the
                      others are listed as what they are — the same test, seen under another
                      description — rather than one being promoted to the label and the rest
                      demoted to history that was never measured.

                      Nullable, and empty rather than nil: `names` is `ARRAY_AGG(…) FILTER (…)`, so
                      a test every run reported anonymously comes back with none. A blank cell in a
                      ranked list is a row the reader can neither identify nor go and find, so that
                      row says so and leans on the file line below it. %>
                  <% if test.descriptions.any? %>
                    <%= test.descriptions.first %>
                  <% else %>
                    <span class="text-app-muted">no description reported</span>
                  <% end %>
                  <%# ⭐ THE REWORDED TEST, VISIBLE. Its description changed while its identity did
                      not, so its history did not restart — and this is the disclosure the outcome
                      panel above structurally cannot make, since grouped on `name` a reword is two
                      tests there. %>
                  <% if test.renamed? %>
                    <span class="block text-xs text-app-muted">
                      also recorded as <%= test.descriptions.drop(1).to_sentence %>
                    </span>
                  <% end %>
                  <%# ⭐ THE MOVED TEST, VISIBLE — and the single sentence this whole read exists
                      for. Per the project's identity rule a test that moved is the same test and
                      keeps its runtime history, so this is the guarantee being KEPT rather than an
                      anomaly to flag; the reader is told because a history spanning two files is a
                      fact about where to go looking. The files are named rather than counted, for
                      the reason the outcome panel names its own: "recorded under 2 files" sends a
                      reader looking without saying where.

                      The file that RAN the test (`spec_file_path`), which for a shared example
                      group is not the file that defines it. Nullable in the schema, so a test whose
                      every row reported none says so rather than rendering a blank. %>
                  <%# EVERY file named here is a drill-in, not just the first. The value on screen is
                      `ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT spec_file_path)` off the same column
                      `SpecObservation.in_file` is keyed on, so the string a reader sees IS the key
                      the destination panel consumes — no projection widening and no lookup.

                      Every entry of a MOVED row is linkable, including a path only the older runs
                      of the window recorded while the destination is anchored on the newest run.
                      That is an ordinary answer rather than an error — `SpecFileExamples` states it
                      in its own class comment and the destination renders its named empty state —
                      and it is what makes the one disclosure this read exists for navigable on BOTH
                      of its files rather than on whichever one happened to sort first.

                      Through `drill_down_path`, so opening a file from here does not close a branch,
                      an area or a description the reader opened separately, nor re-anchor them to
                      the newest run. %>
                  <span class="block font-mono text-xs text-app-muted">
                    <% if test.files_seen.any? %>
                      <% links = test.files_seen.map do |path|
                           link_to path,
                                   drill_down_path(@repository, spec_file: path,
                                                   anchor: "spec-file-examples"),
                                   class: "underline underline-offset-2 decoration-app-border-light " \
                                          "text-app-content hover:decoration-app-content",
                                   aria: { current: path == @spec_file_request ? "true" : nil }
                         end %>
                      <% if test.moved? %>
                        <%# `Array#to_sentence` returns a plain String and NOT a SafeBuffer, so the
                            joined markup has to be marked safe or ERB renders visible `&lt;a href=`.
                            It is safe to do here because every element is `link_to` output, which
                            escapes both the href and the body, and the only thing `to_sentence`
                            itself contributes is its own ", " / " and " connectors — no unescaped
                            value from a run reaches the output. `safe_join`, this codebase's usual
                            way of joining buffers, is passed over deliberately: it has no "and"
                            connector, and the sentence reading is the whole point of this line. %>
                        recorded under <%= links.to_sentence.html_safe %>
                      <% else %>
                        <%= links.first %>
                      <% end %>
                    <% else %>
                      not reported
                    <% end %>
                  </span>
                </td>
                <%# The window TOTAL — what this test cost the suite across every run of the window
                    — through `SpecObservation.humanized_duration`, which is what keeps a group
                    nothing timed reading "not reported" rather than `0.00s`. A zero there would be
                    a measurement invented out of silence, and it would sort as the cheapest test in
                    the suite. %>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content tabular-nums"><%= test.duration_label %></td>
                <%# ⭐ The single longest run of it, BESIDE the total and never instead of it. Sixty
                    seconds is one minute-long test or sixty runs of a cheap one, and a ranking
                    ordered on the sum alone cannot tell a reader which they are looking at — which
                    is the difference between a test to optimise and a suite to run less often. The
                    column is ordered on the total, not on this, and the caption says so. %>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                  <%= test.slowest_label %>
                </td>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                  <%# Always the fraction. "7" alone reads as seven of something unstated, and here
                      the denominator IS part of the claim: a test seen in 7 of 30 runs has a total
                      summed over less than a quarter of the window it is being ranked across. %>
                  <%= test.appearance_label(@slowest_tests.run_count) %>
                  <%# An identity is not a key WITHIN a run — a table-driven loop, a shared example
                      group and two verbatim-identical tests are one identity and several rows —
                      so this is what separates "slow in 12 runs" from "run three times in each of
                      four". Without it the total above cannot be attributed to either. %>
                  <% if test.repeated_within_run? %>
                    <span class="block text-xs text-app-muted">
                      <%= number_with_delimiter(test.recorded_count) %> rows, so it ran more than
                      once in at least one of them
                    </span>
                  <% end %>
                </td>
                <%# How much of THIS test's own history carried a timing — its own denominator, not
                    the panel's. The total above is a `SUM`, and `SUM` skips nulls silently, so a
                    row totalling four of its nine appearances has to say so or the figure is a
                    measurement over a population the reader cannot see. %>
                <td class="px-3 py-2 text-app-content-secondary tabular-nums">
                  <%= test.coverage_label %>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        <% when :unresolved %>
          <%# ⭐ THE STATE THIS PANEL EXISTS TO GET RIGHT. The newest run wrote per-example rows and
              not one of them has been matched to a durable test yet, which is what every run looks
              like for the seconds after it lands. An empty ranking here would be read as "nothing
              in this suite is slow"; it is "nobody has told us which tests these are", and only the
              wording separates them. %>
          <div id="slowest-tests-window-unresolved">
            <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                  title: "These runs' tests have not been identified yet",
                  description: slowest_tests_unresolved_description(@slowest_tests)
                ) %>
          </div>
        <% when :unrecorded %>
          <%# A different absence entirely, and one nothing is going to clear on its own: the newest
              run wrote NO per-example rows, so there is no per-test grain in this window to rank at
              any identity. A run ingested before those rows existed, or one whose client reports
              only totals, is this state — a fact about the payload rather than about the suite, and
              the same line the single-run panels above draw with their own `recorded?`. %>
          <div id="slowest-tests-window-unrecorded">
            <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                  title: "No per-test detail in this window",
                  description: "#{@slowest_tests.anchor_run.commit_sha.first(7)}, the newest run " \
                               "in this window, reported no per-example detail at all, so there " \
                               "is no per-test runtime here to rank across runs. A run reports a " \
                               "suite size and a duration without reporting its examples, and a " \
                               "client that sends only totals produces exactly this. It is a fact " \
                               "about what CI sent rather than about how fast this suite is."
                ) %>
          </div>
        <% when :no_runs %>
          <%# Not reachable from `RepositoriesController#show`, and rendered rather than omitted.
              The assignment sits inside `if trajectory_runs.any?`, so the object this page holds
              always has an anchor — but the state is one of the four `SlowestTests` defines, a
              `case` that dropped it would render a titled panel with nothing whatever inside it,
              and the guard that makes it unreachable is in a different file from this one. Every
              figure this branch could print is `nil` by `SlowestTests::UNREAD`, so it prints none. %>
          <div id="slowest-tests-window-no-runs">
            <%= render UI::EmptyStateComponent.new(
                  title: "No runs in this window yet",
                  description: "There is no run on this branch to rank tests across, so nothing " \
                               "here has been measured. This panel fills in as soon as CI reports."
                ) %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
